{
  "dom_id": "step-e8243",
  "name": "Example: Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes",
  "problem": "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14158570/assets/4733d066bd04d8bbc7fb8203694452b84fd8000b1e616d7afcfad3351ce9e27d.svg)",
  "worked_solution": "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}(x-1)^2.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2(x-1) \\\\  & =3(x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3(x-1)= 0\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[0,2],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, 2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1, 2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)^{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0 ) = f( 2) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"step-e8243\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block;\">\n    \n                 \n                <div class=\"example\">\n                    <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                        <div class=\"stepName\"><span class=\"stepType\">Example:</span> Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes</div>       \n                        <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"e8243\">?</div>\n                    </div>\n            \n                    <div class=\"exampleQuestion\">\n                        <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                           